---
/**
 * CAREER — /career/
 *
 * ═══ WHAT THIS PAGE IS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * A wall of the school's own recruitment posters, each one enlargeable. The
 * client was offered three treatments — a structured vacancy board with the
 * poster attached, this poster wall, and a single featured role over an archive
 * — and chose the wall, with all ten posters published as-is. So the posters ARE
 * the content here; nothing on the page restates or summarises them.
 *
 * ⚠ THIS ROUTE WAS IN THE NAVIGATION WITH NO PAGE BEHIND IT. `/career/` is a
 * top-level main-nav item (navigation.ts §6: "The Career section should be
 * available directly on the main navigation menu for easier accessibility to
 * prospective applicants") and is also in the footer utility list. Until now
 * both landed on the generated "page coming" placeholder. Writing this file
 * retires that placeholder automatically — pages/[...slug].astro only mints
 * routes that have no file.
 *
 * ═══ THE THREE THINGS THAT MAKE A POSTER WALL WORK ═════════════════════════
 *
 * ⚠ 1 · NOTHING IS CROPPED, BECAUSE A CROP CUTS OFF A PHONE NUMBER. These ten
 * files run from 0.67 (job-1, 1365×2048) to 1.32 (job-5, 1024×778) — a poster
 * twice as tall as it is wide next to one that is wider than tall. Forced into
 * a uniform card, half of them lose their bottom edge, and the bottom edge is
 * exactly where every one of them prints the email address and the phone
 * number. So the wall is CSS multi-column with `break-inside: avoid`: each
 * poster keeps its own aspect ratio, the columns end ragged, and not one
 * contact detail is cropped away. Ragged is the correct outcome here, not a
 * defect to be tidied.
 *
 * ⚠ 2 · EVERY POSTER IS TRANSCRIBED INTO ITS alt. Every word of this page's
 * real content — role, subject, salary, qualification, phone number — is pixels
 * inside a JPEG. Untranscribed, this page is blank to Google and to a screen
 * reader: a teacher searching "PGT vacancy Ballia" would never reach it, and a
 * blind applicant would hear "image" ten times. The transcriptions live in
 * data/career.ts, cost nothing on screen, and are the only reason this page is
 * findable. This was going to be true whichever treatment was chosen.
 *
 * ⚠ 3 · THE POSTERS DISAGREE ABOUT HOW TO APPLY, SO THE PAGE ANSWERS ONCE.
 * Between them the ten print five different phone numbers and two different
 * email addresses. An applicant should not have to work out which is current
 * from a wall of JPEGs, so "How to apply" below states one route in real text —
 * the recruitment inbox the client chose, with the school office as the spoken
 * fallback. The posters still say whatever they say; the page does not pretend
 * otherwise, it just does not leave the choice to the reader.
 *
 * ═══ MOTION ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * Everything on this page enters on scroll through the site's own contract in
 * scripts/motion-gsap.ts — no page-local animation code, so this page cannot
 * drift from the rest of the site's timing, easing or reduced-motion handling.
 * Three attributes do all of it: `data-split` (the heading arrives word by word
 * from under a mask), `data-reveal` (a calm 26px fade-up, batched so everything
 * entering together shares one stagger) and `data-pop` (the louder cousin —
 * further up, slight overshoot, so a card lands rather than fades).
 *
 * ⚠ WHICH ELEMENT CARRIES THE ATTRIBUTE IS A REAL DECISION, NOT A DETAIL. A
 * finished GSAP tween leaves an inline `transform` on its target, and an inline
 * transform outranks the stylesheet — so it silently kills any `:hover`
 * transform on the SAME node. This project has already lost the affiliation
 * tiles' hover lift to exactly that. Two consequences here:
 *
 *   · The wall's `data-reveal` sits on `.wall__it` (the <li>), NOT on `.wall__a`
 *     — the anchor is what lifts 4px on hover, and it has to stay untouched.
 *   · The apply rows carry `data-pop`, which clears its props when it finishes,
 *     so `.how__row:hover`'s 2px lift survives on the very element that animated.
 *
 * ⚠ THE WALL IS BATCHED PER ITEM, NOT TRIGGERED AS ONE BLOCK. Ten posters make a
 * column several screens tall; a single trigger on the container would fire all
 * ten the moment its top edge appeared, and the seven still below the fold would
 * finish animating unseen and be sitting there flat by the time a reader reached
 * them. `data-reveal` uses ScrollTrigger.batch, so each poster arrives as it
 * actually comes into view.
 *
 * ⚠ THREE POSTERS ARE OUT OF DATE AND THE CLIENT CHOSE TO KEEP THEM. job-5,
 * job-6 and job-7 advertise walk-in interviews from Dec 2023 to Jan 2025. This
 * was raised before building; the client's instruction was to publish all ten
 * as-is. They are sorted to the end of the wall and their caption states the
 * campaign date, so a reader sees WHEN before planning a journey to Agarsanda.
 * That is the most this treatment can honestly do — see data/career.ts.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Lightbox from "../components/ui/Lightbox.astro";
import Backdrop from "../components/ui/Backdrop.astro";
import heroPhoto from "../assets/photos/sunbeem-1.jpg";
import applicant from "../assets/icons/thinkgirl.png";
import CmsImage from "../components/ui/CmsImage.astro";
import { getCareerPageData, getCareerCopy, fileUrl, getSchool } from '../lib/cms';

const school = await getSchool();

/* ⚠ FROM STRAPI NOW, NOT data/career.ts. `posterCount` stays DERIVED from the
   wall itself rather than stored — a stored number disagrees with the wall the
   first time somebody adds a poster and forgets to update it. The old data file
   remains on disk until this is verified in a production build. */
const { posters, posterCount } = await getCareerPageData();
const copy = await getCareerCopy();

/**
 * ⚠ THE ROW ICONS BELOW ARE THE CONTACT PAGE'S, GLYPH FOR GLYPH. Envelope,
 * handset and map pin are already drawn on /contact-us/'s address panel, at the
 * same size on the same rounded tile. A school site that draws two different
 * envelopes two clicks apart looks assembled rather than designed, so these are
 * the same paths rather than new ones. They are inlined here because the project
 * has no icon component — the contact panel inlines its three the same way, and
 * inventing an abstraction for six glyphs across two pages would be the larger
 * change.
 */

/**
 * ⚠ THE WIDTH LIST IS GONE, AND THE REASON IT EXISTED IS NOW HANDLED UPSTREAM.
 * It used to read [420, 720, 1080] — sized for the LIGHTBOX rather than the
 * thumbnail, because every frame here is a lightbox trigger and a reader
 * enlarging a poster to read a phone number is the entire interaction. Strapi
 * derives its sizes once at upload from the breakpoints in
 * apps/cms/config/plugins.ts, which run to 1600, and the lightbox opens the
 * original file regardless. WALL_SIZES stays: the browser still has to be told
 * how wide the slot is.
 */
/**
 * ⚠ THE ADDRESS IS SPLIT SO IT HAS SOMEWHERE SENSIBLE TO BREAK. 34 characters
 * with no space in them: on a 360px phone there is no font size at which it fits
 * one line and none at which the card can give up the room, so the only question
 * left is WHERE it wraps. Left to the browser it broke as "…gmail.co / m" — a
 * stray letter on its own line, on the one link this page exists to be clicked.
 * A <wbr> before the @ turns that into "appointmentsunbeamballia / @gmail.com",
 * which reads as a decision.
 *
 * ⚠ IT ONLY WORKS BECAUSE `.how__v` USES `overflow-wrap: break-word`, NOT
 * `anywhere`. `anywhere` breaks greedily at whatever character fills the line and
 * ignores the hint entirely; `break-word` takes the offered opportunity first and
 * only breaks mid-token when there is none. Both still guarantee the address
 * cannot overflow the card — that is the property neither one gives up.
 */
const [mailUser, mailDomain] = school.recruitmentEmail.split('@');

const WALL_SIZES = "(min-width: 1024px) 32vw, (min-width: 640px) 46vw, 92vw";

/**
 * ⚠ job-4 IS 526×526 AND THAT IS ALL THERE IS. It is by far the smallest file in
 * the set and its body copy is small even at native size, so enlarging it will
 * look soft where the others look sharp. Astro will not invent detail that is
 * not in the source, and upscaling it to match the others would only make the
 * softness worse. Flagged rather than hidden: if the school still has the
 * artwork, a re-export at 1080px wide is a drop-in replacement for the file.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
>
  <PageHero
    src={heroPhoto}
  />

  <div class="u-page-panel cr">
    <div class="u-container">
      {
        /* ── The opening line, and the apply route stated once ─────────────── */
      }
      <div class="cr__head">
        <p class="cr__eyebrow" data-reveal>
          <span class="cr__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {
            /* The count comes from the data, so it can never drift from the wall
              below the way a typed number would. */
          }
          {posterCount} postings
        </p>
        {/* ⚠ AN ELEMENT CHILD INSIDE data-split IS SAFE, AND THAT IS BY DESIGN
            IN THE SPLITTER. splitWords() walks child NODES rather than reading
            textContent precisely so headings can carry a plate — it splits text
            nodes into words and passes an element through whole, wrapped in
            `.sp-mask--free` (overflow visible) so it animates as its own unit
            without being sliced by a mask. Same treatment `.t-squiggle` gets on
            the history page.

            ⚠ WHICH IS ALSO WHY THIS HEADING IS NOT `display: flex` the way the
            apply card's is. The splitter replaces the heading's children with a
            run of inline-block masks separated by text-node spaces; in a flex
            container each of those spaces becomes an anonymous flex item and the
            spacing goes wrong. Inline-block plus a margin is what survives the
            rebuild. */}
        <h2 class="cr__h" data-split><span class="starmark cr__star" aria-hidden="true"></span>{copy.wall.heading}</h2>
        <p class="cr__stand" data-reveal>
          {copy.wall.stand}
        </p>

        {
          /* ⚠ TWO ROUTES, LABELLED, AND THAT WAS THE CLIENT'S CHOICE. The email is
            the primary action because every poster in the set asks for a CV by
            email; the number is the spoken fallback for anyone applying for the
            driver or conductor role, whose poster asks candidates to bring their
            licence to the office rather than to write. */
        }
        <div class="cr__apply" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="120">
          <a
            class="cr__cta"
            href={`mailto:${school.recruitmentEmail}?subject=${encodeURIComponent("Application — Sunbeam School Ballia")}`}
          >
            <svg
              width="17"
              height="17"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <rect
                x="3"
                y="5.5"
                width="18"
                height="13"
                rx="2.4"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"></rect>
              <path
                d="m4 7 8 5.6L20 7"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"
                stroke-linecap="round"></path>
            </svg>
            {copy.ctaCvLabel}
          </a>

          <a class="cr__cta cr__cta--ghost" href={`tel:${school.phone.office}`}>
            <svg
              width="17"
              height="17"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <path
                d="M7.6 3.5h-2A2.1 2.1 0 0 0 3.5 5.8C3.5 13.7 10.3 20.5 18.2 20.5a2.1 2.1 0 0 0 2.3-2.1v-2a1.3 1.3 0 0 0-1-1.3l-3-.7a1.3 1.3 0 0 0-1.3.5l-.9 1.2a12.6 12.6 0 0 1-5.4-5.4l1.2-.9a1.3 1.3 0 0 0 .5-1.3l-.7-3a1.3 1.3 0 0 0-1.3-1Z"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.8"
                stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
            </svg>
            {copy.ctaCallLabel}
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      {
        /* ═══ THE WALL ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
          ⚠ A LIST, NOT A ROW OF DIVS. Ten sibling frames with no grouping are
          ten unrelated images to a screen reader; a list announces "list, 10
          items" and gives the reader somewhere to skip to.

          ⚠ THE COLUMN COUNT IS THE ONLY THING THAT CHANGES AT A BREAKPOINT.
          Multi-column flows the items itself, so there is no per-item
          positioning to keep in step — which is exactly why it survives ten
          posters of ten different heights where a fixed grid would not. */
      }
      <ul class="wall">
        {
          posters.map((p, i) => (
            <li class="wall__it" data-reveal>
              {/* ⚠ AN <a href> TO THE FILE, NOT A <button>. This is the
                  Lightbox's contract: with JavaScript off the link still opens
                  the poster itself, so the reader loses the viewer and keeps the
                  picture. A button would leave them a control that does nothing
                  — on the one page where the content IS the pictures. */}
              <a
                class="wall__a"
                href={fileUrl(p.image)}
                data-lb-open
                data-lb-alt={p.alt}
                data-lb-cap={p.dated ? `${p.title} — ${p.dated}` : p.title}
              >
                {/* ⚠ ONLY THE FIRST THREE ARE EAGER. They are what a reader sees
                  before scrolling; the rest wait. Ten posters at full size is
                  several megabytes, and this page is one a candidate is likely
                  to open on a phone. */}
                <CmsImage
                  file={p.image}
                  alt={p.alt}
                  sizes={WALL_SIZES}
                  fallbackWidth={760}
                  loading={i < 3 ? "eager" : "lazy"}
                  fetchpriority={i === 0 ? "high" : undefined}
                />

                <span class="wall__zoom" aria-hidden="true">
                  <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 18 18" fill="none">
                    <circle
                      cx="8"
                      cy="8"
                      r="5.6"
                      stroke="currentColor"
                      stroke-width="1.7"
                    />
                    <path
                      d="M12.4 12.4L16.5 16.5M8 5.8v4.4M5.8 8h4.4"
                      stroke="currentColor"
                      stroke-width="1.7"
                      stroke-linecap="round"
                    />
                  </svg>
                </span>
              </a>

              {/* ⚠ THE CAPTION IS aria-hidden AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Its every
                  word is already in the link's alt, which a screen reader has
                  just read; leaving it exposed would announce the title, the tag
                  and the date a second time on every one of the ten items. */}
              <div class="wall__cap" aria-hidden="true">
                <p class="wall__t">{p.title}</p>
                <p class="wall__m">
                  <span class="wall__tag">{p.tag}</span>
                  {/* Stated on the card, not only inside the poster, so nobody
                      plans a journey around a walk-in that has passed. */}
                  {p.dated && <span class="wall__when">{p.dated}</span>}
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      {
        /* ═══ HOW TO APPLY ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
          The one place on the page where the route in is written in real text
          rather than printed inside a JPEG.

          ⚠ THE THREE ROUTES STACK NOW, WHERE THEY USED TO SIT THREE-ACROSS. The
          figure needs a lane at the right-hand end, and taking a third of the
          width away from a three-column strip left each route about twenty
          characters wide — the email address alone would have wrapped over three
          lines. Stacked, each route gets a full measure for its note, and the
          panel grows tall enough for the figure to stand against something. */
      }
      <section class="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
        {
          /* ⚠ A CLIPPING WRAPPER, NOT THE CURVES DIRECTLY. Backdrop's own
            `overflow: hidden` clips to a RECTANGLE, so dropped straight into
            this card the strokes would run square across all four rounded
            corners. This layer carries `border-radius: inherit` and does the
            clipping the card's shape needs.

            It cannot be the card itself: `.how` has to stay `overflow: visible`
            or the figure standing over the bottom edge is cut off at the ankles.
            Same reasoning, same solution as AffiliationDetails' `.aff__floor`. */
        }
        <span class="how__bg" aria-hidden="true">
          <Backdrop tone="light" />
        </span>

        <div class="how__body">
          {
            /* ⚠ THE STARS ARE A MASKED SPAN, NOT AN <img>, AND THE FILE FORCED IT.
              Measured, every opaque pixel in two-stars.png is rgb(254,244,234) —
              an almost-white cream, evidently drawn for a dark ground. Placed as
              an image on this white card it renders at about 1.03:1 against the
              paper: technically present, visually nothing. Masking a warm
              gradient through its alpha channel keeps the artwork exactly as
              drawn and gives it ink that can be seen. Same technique, same
              reason, as BurstCard does with shape-2.png.

              ⚠ aria-hidden BECAUSE IT SITS INSIDE THE <h2>. Anything in a heading
              joins the heading's accessible name, and a screen-reader user
              navigating by heading should hear "How to apply", not "star How to
              apply". A span with no text contributes nothing anyway; the
              attribute states the intent. */
          }
          {/* The site's own eyebrow, so this block opens the way every other
              section on the site opens rather than starting cold on a heading. */}
          <p class="how__eyebrow" data-reveal>
            <span class="pill pill--violet">Applications</span>
          </p>

          <h2 class="how__h" id="how-h">
            <span class="starmark how__star" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {copy.apply.heading}
          </h2>

          {/* ⚠ NOT A CLAIM, A SIGNPOST. Everything factual on this card is read
              off data/site.ts; this line only counts the routes below it, which
              is why it can be written here without inventing anything. */}
          <p class="how__lede" data-reveal>{copy.apply.stand}</p>

          {
            /* ⚠ A <dl>, NOT DIVS. Each row is a term and its value — "By email" →
              the address — which is precisely what a description list is for,
              and it is the pattern AffiliationDetails already uses for the same
              shape. The wrapping <div> around each dt/dd pair is valid HTML5 and
              is what lets a row be a grid item. */
          }
          {
            /* ⚠ EVERY TILE IS aria-hidden. The glyph says "this is an email
              address" at a glance, but the row's own <dt> already says "By
              email" and the href says it again to assistive tech — a third
              announcement per row would be noise. */
          }
          {/* ⚠ THE NUMERALS ARE aria-hidden AND ORNAMENTAL. The <dl> already
              conveys "three items" to a screen reader; hearing "01" before every
              label is the count read twice. Same reasoning, and the same tabular
              treatment, as BurstCard's index badge. */}
          <dl class="how__list">
            {/* `--is-primary` on the email route: every poster in the set asks
                for a CV by email, so that route carries the accent and the other
                two are alternatives to it. */}
            <div class="how__row how__row--primary" data-pop>
              <span class="how__no t-tabular" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
              <span class="how__ico" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="19" height="19" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                  <rect
                    x="3"
                    y="5.5"
                    width="18"
                    height="13"
                    rx="2.4"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.8"></rect>
                  <path
                    d="m4 7 8 5.6L20 7"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.8"
                    stroke-linecap="round"></path>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <div class="how__pair">
                <dt class="how__k">{copy.applyMethods[0].label}</dt>
                <dd class="how__d">
                  <a class="how__v" href={`mailto:${school.recruitmentEmail}`}
                    >{mailUser}<wbr />@{mailDomain}</a
                  >
                  <p class="how__n">{copy.applyMethods[0].note}</p>
                </dd>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="how__row" data-pop>
              <span class="how__no t-tabular" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
              <span class="how__ico" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="19" height="19" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                  <path
                    d="M7.6 3.5h-2A2.1 2.1 0 0 0 3.5 5.8C3.5 13.7 10.3 20.5 18.2 20.5a2.1 2.1 0 0 0 2.3-2.1v-2a1.3 1.3 0 0 0-1-1.3l-3-.7a1.3 1.3 0 0 0-1.3.5l-.9 1.2a12.6 12.6 0 0 1-5.4-5.4l1.2-.9a1.3 1.3 0 0 0 .5-1.3l-.7-3a1.3 1.3 0 0 0-1.3-1Z"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.8"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <div class="how__pair">
                <dt class="how__k">{copy.applyMethods[1].label}</dt>
                <dd class="how__d">
                  <a class="how__v" href={`tel:${school.phone.office}`}
                    >{school.phone.officeDisplay}</a
                  >
                  <p class="how__n">{copy.applyMethods[1].note}</p>
                </dd>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="how__row" data-pop>
              <span class="how__no t-tabular" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
              <span class="how__ico" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="19" height="19" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
                  <path
                    d="M12 21s7-6.2 7-11a7 7 0 1 0-14 0c0 4.8 7 11 7 11Z"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.8"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
                  <circle
                    cx="12"
                    cy="10"
                    r="2.6"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.8"></circle>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <div class="how__pair">
                <dt class="how__k">{copy.applyMethods[2].label}</dt>
                <dd class="how__d">
                  {
                    /* <address> is italic by default in every browser, which reads
                      as an aside rather than as the thing the row is for. */
                  }
                  <address class="how__v how__v--plain">
                    {school.address.line1}, {school.address.city}, {
                      school.address.state
                    }
                    {school.address.pin}
                  </address>
                  {/* ⚠ THE LANDMARK COMES FROM SITE SETTINGS, the sentence after
                      it from the CMS. Two owners, one line, on purpose — the
                      landmark is a contact detail printed in many places. */}
                  <p class="how__n">{school.address.landmark}. {copy.applyMethods[2].note}</p>
                </dd>
              </div>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>

        {
          /* ═══ THE FIGURE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
            ⚠ DECORATIVE, SO alt="" AND aria-hidden. It is a stock 3-D character,
            not a photograph of anyone at the school, and it states nothing the
            copy beside it does not. Announcing "boy with laptop" between the
            heading and the three ways to apply would be noise in the one place
            on this page where an applicant is trying to act.

            ⚠ IT IS THE ONLY IMAGE ON THIS PAGE THAT IS NOT A POSTER, and that is
            worth saying out loud: the wall above is the school's own artwork, so
            an illustrated character here must read as ornament and never as a
            tenth-and-a-half job notice. It sits outside the panel's content
            column and carries no caption for exactly that reason. */
        }
        <div class="how__fig" aria-hidden="true" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="180">
          <span class="how__glow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <Image
            src={applicant}
            alt=""
            widths={[240, 480]}
            sizes="240px"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    /* One viewer for the whole wall. Dropped in once; every `data-lb-open`
      above binds to it. */
  }
  <Lightbox />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ═══ HEAD ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .cr__head {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-bottom: clamp(30px, 4vw, 52px);
  }

  .cr__eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 9px;
    padding: 7px 15px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--sb-violet-wash);
    /* The dark step, not the brand orange: at 12px uppercase these count as
       small text, and the brighter orange measures under 4.5:1 on this wash. */
    color: var(--sb-violet-dark);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .cr__dot {
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--sb-violet);
  }

  .cr__h {
    margin-top: clamp(16px, 2vw, 22px);
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 58px);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
  }

  .cr__stand {
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin-top: clamp(12px, 1.6vw, 18px);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.15vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--sb-text-secondary);
  }

  /* ── the two apply routes ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `wrap` because the pair overflows a 320px phone on one line, and a button
     half off the screen is worse than two stacked. */
  .cr__apply {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 28px);
  }

  .cr__cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    min-height: 24px;
    padding: 14px 24px;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--sb-violet);
    color: #fff; /* 5.08:1 */
    font-size: 14.5px;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      background 200ms ease,
      transform 240ms var(--sb-ease),
      border-color 200ms ease;
  }

  .cr__cta:hover {
    background: var(--sb-violet-dark);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }
  .cr__cta:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--sb-focus);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .cr__cta--ghost {
    background: transparent;
    border-color: var(--sb-border);
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
  }

  .cr__cta--ghost:hover {
    background: var(--sb-violet-wash);
    border-color: var(--sb-violet);
    color: var(--sb-violet-dark);
  }

  /* ═══ THE WALL ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ⚠ MULTI-COLUMN, NOT GRID, AND THE REASON IS THE ARTWORK. See the note at the
     head of this file: these posters run 0.67 to 1.32 in aspect ratio, and any
     layout that imposes a shared height crops the contact details off the foot
     of the tall ones. Columns let every frame keep its own proportion.

     ⚠ `break-inside: avoid` IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DEFENSIVE. Without it a poster
     splits across a column boundary — the top half at the foot of one column and
     the bottom half at the head of the next. */
  .wall {
    columns: 1;
    column-gap: clamp(16px, 2vw, 26px);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .wall__it {
    break-inside: avoid;
    /* The vertical rhythm of the wall. `margin-bottom` rather than a row gap,
       because in a multi-column flow there are no rows to gap. */
    margin-bottom: clamp(16px, 2vw, 26px);
  }

  .wall__a {
    display: block;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--sb-border);
    border-radius: clamp(14px, 1.4vw, 20px);
    /* The posters are mostly light artwork; a white ground behind the frame
       keeps a pale poster from dissolving into the ivory panel. */
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 14px 40px -30px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.44);
    transition:
      transform 320ms var(--sb-ease),
      box-shadow 320ms var(--sb-ease),
      border-color 320ms var(--sb-ease);
  }

  .wall__a:hover {
    transform: translateY(-4px);
    border-color: var(--sb-violet);
    box-shadow: 0 26px 60px -32px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.5);
  }

  .wall__a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--sb-focus);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* ⚠ `display: block` ON THE PICTURE AND THE IMG. `<picture>` is an inline box,
     and an inline box sits on the text baseline — which leaves a few pixels of
     stray background under every poster in the wall. This project has hit that
     fault three times; it is cheaper to state it than to rediscover it. */
  .wall__a :global(picture),
  .wall__a :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* The magnifier, so the frame reads as openable rather than as a picture that
     happens to be a link. */
  .wall__zoom {
    position: absolute;
    right: 10px;
    bottom: 10px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: rgba(18, 12, 8, 0.72);
    color: #fff;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(6px);
    transition:
      opacity 240ms var(--sb-ease),
      transform 240ms var(--sb-ease);
  }

  /* ⚠ SHOWN ON FOCUS TOO, NOT HOVER ALONE. Tabbing through the wall with a
     keyboard should give the same affordance a mouse gets. */
  .wall__a:hover .wall__zoom,
  .wall__a:focus-visible .wall__zoom {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }

  /* ── caption ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Outside the frame rather than over it. Laid over the poster it would cover
     artwork that is already dense with type, and every poster's foot is where
     the contact details are printed. */
  .wall__cap {
    padding: 12px 4px 0;
  }

  .wall__t {
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.32;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
  }

  .wall__m {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px 10px;
    margin-top: 7px;
  }

  .wall__tag {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sb-text-secondary);
  }

  /* ⚠ THE DATE IS THE ONE THING ON THIS CARD THAT IS ALLOWED TO SHOUT. It is
     the difference between a reader applying and a reader driving to Agarsanda
     for a walk-in that happened in 2023. Amber rather than red: this is a note
     about age, not an error. */
  .wall__when {
    padding: 3px 9px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(180, 83, 9, 0.28);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgba(180, 83, 9, 0.07);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: #9a3412; /* 6.1:1 on the ivory panel */
  }

  /* ═══ HOW TO APPLY ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ⚠ `--how-pad` IS A VARIABLE BECAUSE TWO RULES HAVE TO AGREE ON IT. The
     figure stands on the panel's floor by cancelling the panel's own bottom
     padding with a negative margin. Written as two separate clamps those would
     drift apart the first time either was touched, and the drift would be a
     character floating a few pixels above the ground — visible, and baffling to
     whoever inherits it. */
  /* ⚠ A TINTED GROUND, NOT FLAT WHITE, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE ROWS READ AS
     CARDS. White rows on a white panel are not cards — they are text with a
     border drawn round it, which is why this block looked plain. Warming the
     panel to a cream that deepens toward the foot gives the three white routes
     something to sit ON, and gives the figure at the right a ground to stand on
     rather than a void. The stops are the site's own: ivory into sand. */
  .how {
    --how-pad: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px);
    position: relative;
    margin-top: clamp(40px, 5vw, 72px);
    padding: var(--how-pad);
    border: 1px solid var(--sb-border);
    border-radius: clamp(18px, 1.8vw, 26px);
    background: linear-gradient(158deg, #fffdfb 0%, var(--sb-ivory) 46%, var(--sb-sand) 100%);
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px -44px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.5);
    /* ⚠ NOT `overflow: hidden`. The figure deliberately breaks the panel's
       bottom edge; clipping it would cut her off at the ankles. */
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* ── the waves ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The clipping layer. Backdrop paints itself into this; the card's content
     sits above it on z-index 1. */
  .how__bg {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 0;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The one job this element exists for — see the note in the markup. */
    border-radius: inherit;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* ⚠ BOTH CHILDREN NEED A STACKING POSITION, NOT JUST ONE. The waves are a
     positioned layer at z-index 0; a static sibling paints below every
     positioned one regardless of source order, so without these the copy and the
     figure would both sit UNDER the strokes. */
  .how__body,
  .how__fig {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .how__eyebrow { margin-bottom: clamp(12px, 1.4vw, 16px); }

  .how__h {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 11px;
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    /* Up from clamp(21px, 2vw, 27px). This is the page's closing section and the
       last thing an applicant reads; at 27px it sat below the poster captions in
       presence and the block read as a footnote. */
    font-size: clamp(26px, 2.7vw, 36px);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.028em;
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
  }

  .how__lede {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-top: clamp(8px, 1vw, 12px);
    font-size: clamp(14.5px, 1.05vw, 16px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--sb-text-secondary);
  }

  /* ═══ THE SPARKLE MARK ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ⚠ ONE RULE, TWO HEADINGS. Both "Work at Sunbeam" and "How to apply" wear it,
     and the ink, the mask and the proportion must never differ between them —
     two subtly different stars on one page is worse than none. Only size and
     placement are left to the modifiers below.

     ⚠ WHY A MASK AND NOT AN <img>: measured, every opaque pixel in
     two-stars.png is rgb(254,244,234), an almost-white cream drawn for a dark
     ground. As an image on these pale panels it renders at about 1.03:1 —
     present, invisible. Painting a gradient through its alpha keeps the artwork
     exactly as drawn and gives it ink that can be seen. Same technique BurstCard
     uses on shape-2.png, for the same reason.

     ⚠ `aspect-ratio: 195 / 246`, THE FILE'S OWN PROPORTION. A masked span has no
     intrinsic size — nothing stops it being set square, and set square this
     artwork stretches by a quarter. */
  .starmark {
    aspect-ratio: 195 / 246;

    /* A warm two-stop lift so the upper sparkle reads brighter than the lower
       one, which is the emphasis the artwork itself is drawn with. */
    background: linear-gradient(150deg, #ffb27a 0%, var(--sb-violet) 78%);

    -webkit-mask-image: url("../assets/icons/two-stars.png");
    mask-image: url("../assets/icons/two-stars.png");
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    /* `contain`, not `cover`: cover would crop the sparkles at the box edges. */
    -webkit-mask-size: contain;
    mask-size: contain;
    -webkit-mask-position: center;
    mask-position: center;
  }

  /* ⚠ THE FALLBACK IS NOTHING, DELIBERATELY. Where mask-image is unsupported the
     gradient would otherwise paint as a solid orange rectangle beside the
     heading — a coloured block where a pair of stars should be, which is far
     worse than an absent ornament. Mask support is near-universal; the one
     browser that lacks it simply gets the headings on their own. */
  @supports not ((mask-image: url("")) or (-webkit-mask-image: url(""))) {
    .starmark {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* On the apply card: a flex item beside the heading text. */
  .how__star {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    height: clamp(26px, 2.6vw, 34px);
  }

  /* ⚠ ON THE PAGE HEADING IT IS INLINE-BLOCK AND SIZED IN `em`, NOT px. This
     heading runs clamp(34px, 5vw, 58px) — a fixed height would be two-thirds of
     the cap at 1440 and taller than the letters at 360. `0.66em` holds one
     relationship to the type at every width, with no second clamp to keep in
     step with the first.

     `vertical-align` is explicit because the splitter wraps this in a mask span
     that sets its own `bottom`; left to the default the star would hang off the
     baseline instead of sitting against the letters. */
  /* ⚠ THE MARGIN IS TINY BECAUSE THE ARTWORK BRINGS ITS OWN. The two sparkles
     sit top-right and bottom-left of the file, which leaves the box's
     bottom-right corner empty — exactly the corner that faces the "W". At
     0.28em the optical gap measured nearer 40px against a 58px cap and the star
     read as detached from the word. 0.06em lands it where 0.28em looks like it
     should have. */
  .cr__star {
    display: inline-block;
    height: 0.72em;
    margin-right: 0.06em;
    vertical-align: -0.06em;
  }

  /* ── the three routes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Stacked at every width. See the note in the markup: the figure takes the
     right-hand third at desktop, and three columns in what is left gives each
     route a measure too narrow for the email address to sit on one line. */
  /* ⚠ CARDS NOW, NOT HAIRLINE-SEPARATED ROWS — this is the change that fixes
     "it looks simple". Three blocks of similar text divided by 1px rules is a
     list; three lifted white cards on a warm ground is a set of choices, which
     is what these three actually are. The gap replaces the rules, so nothing
     draws a line through the middle of the panel any more. */
  .how__list {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(10px, 1.2vw, 14px);
    margin: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 28px) 0 0;
  }

  /* ⚠ THE ICON AND THE NUMERAL ARE THEIR OWN COLUMNS, which is what puts every
     label, value and note on one vertical rule down the card. Inlined into the
     label instead, the three notes would each start at a different x and the
     rows would read as three loose blocks rather than one set. Same icon-column
     construction as the contact page's address panel. */
  /* ⚠ TIGHTENED ON THE CLIENT'S NOTE THAT THE CARDS WERE TOO BIG. Every number
     here came down a step — 42px tile → 36, 20px padding → 15, and the type with
     them below. The proportions are unchanged; the whole set is simply smaller,
     which is what keeps three cards reading as one block rather than three
     panels. */
  .how__row {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 36px minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: start;
    gap: clamp(10px, 1.1vw, 13px);
    padding: clamp(12px, 1.3vw, 15px) clamp(13px, 1.4vw, 17px);
    border: 1px solid var(--sb-border);
    border-radius: clamp(12px, 1.1vw, 15px);
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 10px 26px -22px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.4);
    transition:
      transform 300ms var(--sb-ease),
      box-shadow 300ms var(--sb-ease),
      border-color 300ms var(--sb-ease);
  }

  /* ⚠ THE ACCENT MARKS THE ROUTE THE SCHOOL ACTUALLY ASKS FOR. Every poster in
     the wall above says "send your CV to…"; the phone and the doorstep are
     alternatives to that, not equals of it. A 3px edge is enough to rank the
     three without turning one into a button. */
  .how__row--primary {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sb-violet) 26%, transparent);
    box-shadow:
      inset 3px 0 0 var(--sb-violet),
      0 14px 32px -22px rgba(201, 60, 10, 0.5);
  }

  /* ⚠ THE WHOLE ROW LIFTS ON HOVER THOUGH ONLY THE VALUE IS A LINK, and that is
     deliberate: the card is the target a reader aims at, and a card that ignores
     the pointer while the six words inside it react reads as broken. The lift is
     2px — enough to acknowledge, too little to shift the text under the cursor. */
  .how__row:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sb-violet) 38%, transparent);
    box-shadow: 0 18px 38px -24px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.46);
  }

  .how__row--primary:hover {
    box-shadow:
      inset 3px 0 0 var(--sb-violet),
      0 20px 42px -22px rgba(201, 60, 10, 0.55);
  }

  .how__ico {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border-radius: 11px;
    background: var(--sb-violet-wash);
    color: var(--sb-violet);
    transition: background 300ms var(--sb-ease), color 300ms var(--sb-ease);
  }

  /* The glyph comes down with the tile — a 19px icon in a 36px tile leaves only
     8px of ground and the tile stops reading as a tile. */
  .how__ico svg { width: 17px; height: 17px; }

  /* The tile fills on hover — the one moment of colour in the card, and it lands
     on the glyph that says what the route is. */
  .how__row:hover .how__ico {
    background: var(--sb-violet);
    color: #fff;
  }

  /* ⚠ ORNAMENTAL, AND SIZED TO STAY THAT WAY. Tabular figures so 01/02/03 sit on
     the same rhythm, and pale enough that the numeral never competes with the
     value beside it — it is a marker for the eye scanning the card, not a
     reading order anybody has to follow. */
  .how__no {
    grid-column: 3;
    grid-row: 1;
    align-self: start;
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.5vw, 19px);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sb-violet) 26%, transparent);
  }

  /* The <dt>/<dd> pair, so the row's two columns are icon and body rather than
     icon, term and value. */
  .how__pair {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .how__k {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11.5px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sb-violet-dark);
  }

  /* A <dd> carries a default 40px indent in every browser, which would step all
     three values in from their own labels. */
  .how__d {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .how__v {
    display: block;
    width: fit-content;
    min-height: 24px;
    margin-top: 5px;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.5;
    /* ⚠ `break-word`, NOT `anywhere` — the difference decides WHERE the address
       wraps. An email has no spaces, so without either it would push the card
       wide instead of wrapping; both prevent that. But `anywhere` breaks
       greedily at whatever character happens to fill the line and ignores the
       <wbr> in the markup, which is how "…gmail.co / m" happened. `break-word`
       takes the offered break first, so the address splits at the @ or not at
       all. See the note beside `mailUser` in the frontmatter. */
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
    color: var(--sb-violet);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .how__v:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .how__v:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--sb-focus);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* The address is not a link, so it takes neither the link colour nor the
     underline — it would read as a control that does nothing. */
  .how__v--plain {
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
  }

  .how__n {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-top: 5px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--sb-text-secondary);
  }

  /* ═══ NARROW: THE ICON BECOMES A HEADER, NOT A COLUMN ═════════════════════
     ⚠ THE ICON COLUMN COSTS 54px OF MEASURE, AND ON A PHONE THERE IS NONE TO
     SPARE. The recruitment address is 34 characters with no space in it — the
     one link this page exists to be clicked — and it had only just fitted a
     390px card before the tiles were added. With 42px of tile and a 12px gap
     taken off the front of every row it broke to a second line again, carrying
     "com" on its own.

     Shrinking the type further was the wrong lever: it treats the symptom and
     makes the most important link on the page the smallest. Instead the row
     stops being two columns and becomes two ROWS — icon and label together as a
     header, then the value and its note across the card's full width. The
     address gets back the 54px, and the layout reads better besides.

     `display: contents` on the pair is what allows it: the wrapper's box is
     dropped so the <dt> and <dd> become grid items of the row itself and can be
     placed independently. It has no semantics to lose — it exists only to keep
     the <dl> structure valid. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* ⚠ AND FOUR MORE PIXELS FROM THE PADDING, MEASURED. Full-width was worth
       54px and still left the address one pixel short: it wants 301px and a
       350px card at 24px padding offers 302px — which wrapped, because the
       measurement is not exact to the pixel. 20px padding offers 310px and it
       sits on one line with room to spare. Verified at 390, 430, 560 and 640. */
    .how {
      --how-pad: 20px;
    }

    /* ⚠ THE THIRD COLUMN HAS TO BE DECLARED HERE TOO. `.how__no` is placed at
       `grid-column: 3`; against a two-column template that mints an IMPLICIT
       third column sized by `grid-auto-columns` — which is `auto`, so it works
       by accident and breaks the moment anything else is auto-placed. Stating it
       keeps the numeral in a track the template knows about. */
    .how__row {
      grid-template-columns: 38px minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
      gap: 10px 12px;
      align-items: center;
      padding: 14px 16px;
    }

    .how__pair {
      display: contents;
    }

    .how__ico {
      width: 38px;
      height: 38px;
      border-radius: 12px;
    }

    /* ⚠ THE CARDS TOOK BACK 32px AND THE ADDRESS NO LONGER FITS AT 15.5px.
       Going to cards gave every route 16px of padding on each side, and the
       measure that was carefully won for the recruitment address went with it:
       350px card − 40px panel padding − 32px card padding leaves 278px for a
       301px string. There is no padding left to take — at p + q ≤ 24 the cards
       would have no inner space at all — so this is the one place where the type
       has to give. Fluid rather than a hard 14px: it is only 14px at the very
       narrowest, and back to full size by 430 where the room exists. */
    .how__v {
      font-size: clamp(13.5px, 3.6vw, 15.5px);
    }

    .how__k {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .how__d {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
      /* ⚠ REQUIRED WITH `break-word`. A grid item's default `min-width: auto`
         resolves to min-content, and min-content of an unbreakable address is
         the whole address — which would push the track wider than the card
         rather than wrap inside it. `anywhere` used to mask this by making
         min-content one character wide; `break-word` does not, so the floor has
         to be stated. */
      min-width: 0;
      /* The 9px the value already carries above it is enough once it is on its
         own line; without this the header and the value drift apart. */
      margin-top: -2px;
    }
  }

  /* ── the figure ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     ⚠ HIDDEN BY DEFAULT AND TURNED ON AT THE BREAKPOINT, not the reverse. Below
     900px the panel is a single column of text with no lane to stand her in;
     `display: none` here means the image is never even laid out on a phone,
     where this page is already ten posters long. */
  .how__fig {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    /* Two lanes: the copy, and the figure. `end` so she stands on the panel's
       floor however tall the copy grows. */
    .how {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) clamp(170px, 18vw, 230px);
      gap: clamp(24px, 3vw, 52px);
      align-items: end;
    }

    /* ⚠ THE COPY IS CAPPED, THE PANEL IS NOT. At 1440 the content lane is about
       925px while the longest note wraps at roughly 640px — so the hairline
       between routes ran nearly 300px past the last word, and a rule ending in
       empty space reads as a layout that failed rather than as a divider. The
       cap is above the lane's own width at 1024 (665px), so it changes nothing
       until there is actually slack to take up.

       ⚠ 780, NOT 720 — the icon column moved the text right. Each row now spends
       42px on the tile plus a 16px gap before the copy begins, so the old cap
       would have taken 58px off the measure rather than off the slack. */
    .how__list {
      max-width: 780px;
    }

    /* position/z-index are set in the base rule, above the waves. */
    .how__fig {
      display: block;
      justify-self: center;
    }

    .how__fig :global(img) {
      display: block;
      position: relative;
      z-index: 1;
      width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      /* ⚠ SHE STANDS ON THE PANEL'S EDGE, NOT INSIDE IT. Cancelling the panel's
         own bottom padding puts her feet on the inner floor; the extra 10px
         carries them just over the border, which is what makes her read as
         standing in front of the card rather than pasted inside it. */
      margin-bottom: calc(var(--how-pad) * -1 - 10px);
      user-select: none;
    }

    /* A cut-out PNG dropped on flat white reads as pasted on. A soft warm pool
       behind her feet is what seats her on the panel. */
    .how__glow {
      position: absolute;
      z-index: 0;
      right: -14%;
      bottom: calc(var(--how-pad) * -1);
      left: -14%;
      aspect-ratio: 1;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: radial-gradient(
        circle at 50% 62%,
